                        prompt: "THE BURDEN OF THE PLOW",
                        image: 'images/3_3.png',
                        details: `<p>Foraging was, by the standards of what came next, a decent job\u2014a few hours of skilled searching a day. Farming was clearing, plowing, sowing, weeding, watering, harvesting, threshing, and hauling, from sunrise to sunset. And our bodies were never designed for it: the human spine, knees and neck evolved for walking and climbing, not for stooping over a field.</p>
                        <p>This is one of Harari's sharpest points, and it is easy to miss because it sounds like a complaint about effort. It isn't. It is a point about <strong>evolutionary mismatch</strong>\u2014the same idea that runs through <em>Why Zebras Don't Get Ulcers</em> and <em>Sapiens</em> alike.</p>
                        <p>Our anatomy was shaped over millions of years for a body that walked long distances, climbed, and threw. Within a few thousand years we asked that body to squat, stoop, carry grain sacks and grind flour for twelve hours a day. The archaeological record answers plainly: slipped discs, arthritis, hernias, and worn-out joints appear in farming skeletons and are largely absent from foraging ones. We did not evolve into farmers. We simply started farming, and our bodies paid the difference.</p>`
