                        details: `<p>Foragers moved on and left their waste behind. Farmers stayed\u2014in dense settlements, beside their own rubbish, sharing walls and often rooms with cattle, pigs and chickens. Nearly every great infectious disease of human history, from smallpox to flu to measles, came out of that arrangement: an animal pathogen that found a permanent, crowded, well-connected new host.</p>
                        <p>An epidemic needs three things: a dense population, close contact, and people who stay put long enough to pass an infection along a chain. Nomadic bands of a few dozen supply none of these. A farming village of several hundred, living for generations on the same patch of ground next to its livestock, supplies all three perfectly.</p>
                        <p>The animals mattered most. Measles and tuberculosis have their roots in cattle, influenza in pigs and birds, smallpox in a relative of the cowpox virus. <strong>We invited the diseases in when we invited the herds in.</strong> And once trade routes connected these villages, we had built the world's first infrastructure for pandemics\u2014the same infrastructure, scaled up, that we still live inside today.</p>`
