                        title: 'The Trap of Abundance',
                        body: 'Nobody decided one morning to become a farmer. It was a slow, gradual trap. A band starts cultivating a small patch of wheat. Population grows. They settle down to guard their fields. Now the population is too large to survive by foraging. They are trapped. What started as a small luxury became a necessity that demanded more and more work.',
                        prompt: "THE TRAP OF ABUNDANCE",
                        image: 'images/3_4.png',
                        details: `<p>Nobody decided one morning to become a farmer. It was a slow, gradual trap. A band starts cultivating a small patch of wheat. Population grows. They settle down to guard their fields. Now the population is too large to survive by foraging. They are trapped. What started as a small luxury became a necessity that demanded more and more work.</p>
                        <p>Harari argues the revolution wasn't a conscious choice but a slow, generational trap. It began with small, seemingly harmless decisions. A band might start cultivating a small patch of wheat to supplement their diet. This allowed a slight increase in population.</p>
                        <p>As generations passed, they became more reliant on the crop and settled down to guard their fields. They gave up their nomadic skills. Soon, the population was too large to support by foraging. There was no going back. Harari calls this the <strong>"Luxury Trap"</strong>\u2014what started as a small convenience became an inescapable necessity that demanded ever more work to sustain a larger population at a lower standard of living.</p>
                        <p>The trap is not a historical curiosity; it is the shape of most bad decisions, including yours. Every step is individually reasonable. Each one buys a small improvement. And the improvement quietly raises the floor of what you now need, so you work harder to stay where you were. Harari's warning is that <em>you will never see the trap from inside a single step</em>\u2014only from a distance, looking back at the whole staircase. Notice how neatly this rhymes with the washing machine that was going to give us free time, and the phone that was going to make us reachable.</p>`
