                        prompt: "WHO DOMESTICATED WHO?",
                        image: 'images/3_5.png',
                        details: `<p>Here is Harari\'s most provocative line: from evolution\'s perspective, it wasn\'t Sapiens who domesticated wheat\u2014it was wheat that domesticated Sapiens. Wheat used us as a vehicle to spread from a wild grass in the Middle East to the most widespread plant on Earth. Our DNA multiplied, but our lives got harder.</p>
                        <p>From the viewpoint of our species' DNA, the Agricultural Revolution was a phenomenal success. The number of copies of human DNA multiplied exponentially. But from the viewpoint of individual well-being, it was a disaster.</p>
                        <p>Harari's famous provocation is that <strong>wheat domesticated us</strong>, not the other way around. Before farming, wheat was a wild grass in a small corner of the Middle East. Within a few thousand years, it was growing on every continent. How? By "convincing" Sapiens to clear forests, carry water, weed fields, and spread its seeds. Wheat exploited our desires and turned us into its servants. We became the vehicle for its global domination.</p>`
